  "evidence": "The MDN quote is real and accurately transcribed, but the claim built on top of it overreaches in two ways that both count as refutation grounds. (1) MDN's 'historically been impossible' is a narrow technical statement about genuine cross-document navigation (an actual browser page load/unload) combined with an animated transition — MDN never says anything like 'genuinely new capability rather than a convenience wrapper'; that framing is the claim author's own editorial gloss, not something the primary source states or implies. (2) More substantively, the 'impossible' framing is contradicted by over a decade of shipped practitioner tooling that solved the exact user-facing problem (an animated transition when navigating between different pages/URLs of a multi-page site): Turbolinks (2012+), Barba.js, and Swup all work by intercepting navigation, fetching the new page via AJAX/fetch + the History API, and animating the DOM swap — explicitly to avoid a hard page reload so 'everything that happens on screen is in our control' (per css-tricks.com/page-transitions-for-everyone/, which documents this technique in detail). These libraries were widely used in production specifically to fake cross-document transitions between separate pages before the View Transitions API existed. MDN's silence on this prior art lets the claim read as 'there was no way to get this effect before,' which is false — what's true is narrower: no native API let you get the effect on a *real* full navigation (preserving true multi-page semantics, no-JS fallback, back/forward integrity) without resorting to SPA-style interception hacks. The claim as worded erases that distinction and inflates a real but narrow technical fact ('no native, non-hacky mechanism for animating real cross-document navigations') into a sweeping 'genuinely new capability, not a convenience wrapper' verdict that neither MDN nor the practitioner record cleanly supports.",
  "confidence": "medium",
  "counterSource": "https://css-tricks.com/page-transitions-for-everyone/ (documents Turbolinks/Swup/Barba.js pre-API page-transition techniques); cross-checked against https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/View_Transition_API full paragraph context via WebFetch, which confirms MDN's 'impossible' claim is narrowly scoped and does not itself make the 'not a convenience wrapper' argument"
}
